  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Shifted Unit Step Functions",
    "lesson_core_move": "Translate shifted unit step functions into exact switch-on times on the continuous-time axis so they can be used to start, delay, and construct signals compactly.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Shifted Unit Step Functions",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Shifted Unit Step Functions",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The object to recognize in this step is the unit-step argument. In this topic, the rule is: the switch-on instant is the time that makes the argument of $u(\\cdot)$ equal to zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the base case. The canonical unit step is defined by $u(t)=0$ for $t<0$ and $u(t)=1$ for $t\\ge 0$, so the switch flips on at $t=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(t)=\\begin{cases}0, & t<0\\1, & t\\ge 0\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the shifted form $u(t-T)$, solve $t-T=0$ to get the switch-on time $t=T$. That means $u(t-T)=0$ for $t<T$ and $u(t-T)=1$ for $t\\ge T$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(t-T):\\; t<T \\implies 0,\\; t\\ge T \\implies 1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now contrast with $u(t+T)$. Set $t+T=0$, so the switch occurs at $t=-T$. For positive $T$, this is an advance: the function turns on before zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(t+T):\\; t<-T \\implies 0,\\; t\\ge -T \\implies 1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the same rule for any shift: in $u(t-t_0)$, the switch-on time is $t=t_0$. Do not introduce a new formula here—only change the solve-and-substitute step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Across this lesson, future steps will keep this template unchanged and vary only the inside offset. The action is always: set the inside argument to zero and identify the resulting on-time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Shifted Unit Step Functions",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Finding the Switch-On Time from $u(t - T)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the switch-on time of $y(t)=u(t-3)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the argument inside $u(\\cdot)$: here it is $t-3$. For a shifted step, the function switches on when this argument becomes zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t-3=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Solve the condition directly: $t-3=0 \\implies t=3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t=3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $u(t-3)$ turns on at $t=3$. Therefore the signal is $y(t)=0$ for $t<3$ and $y(t)=1$ for $t\\ge3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}0, & t<3\\\\1, & t\\ge3\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Shifted Unit Step Functions",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding the Switch-On Time from $u(t + T)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the switch-on time of $y(t)=u(t+2)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is still the argument of the unit step: here it is $t+2$. Use the same rule as the previous section and set that argument to zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t+2=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Solve for the switch-on time: $t+2=0 \\implies t=-2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t=-2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So this function switches on at $t=-2$. It is off before the switch and on at and after it: $y(t)=0$ for $t<-2$ and $y(t)=1$ for $t\\ge-2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}0, & t<-2\\\\1, & t\\ge-2\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Shifted Unit Step Functions",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Finding the Switch-On Time from $u(t - t0)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $y(t)=u(t-t_0)$ and $t_0=-1$, find the switch-on time.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shifted argument inside $u(\\cdot)$: here it is $t-t_0$. Use the same rule and set that argument to zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t-t_0=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Rearrange the equation to solve for the switch time: $t=t_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t=t_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the given value $t_0=-1$, so the switch-on time is $t=-1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t=-1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The function is therefore $y(t)=0$ for $t<-1$ and $y(t)=1$ for $t\\ge-1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}0, & t<-1\\\\1, & t\\ge-1\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
